hi I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to  another edition of ask Evan in today's  episode I'm going to answer a question  from one of my newsletter readers Amy  who wrote in asking about the  landscaping company that she's working  for the owner's name is Dave and she had  a couple questions so she wrote in  saying I'm the office manager at this  company I've discovered that I'm not  qualified for this job but I'm trying to  succeed and have a couple questions how  do I begin creating the structure  processes and procedures to the business  so that anyone could walk in and run the  office and manage the employees many  office staff has had turnovers resulted  in total dismantle of our processes and  procedures that Dave the owner has  implemented over the years and then how  do i create a sales compensation plan  that would attract the sales person but  be structures of the company does not  lose money help so Amy thank you for the  question in a really tough situation to  be in you know congrats on on owning up  to the job and trying to make it your  own and be successful at it and for  reaching out for help so to kind of help  answer those questions the first part  about sales sort of the procedure  systems and all that getting that going  for your business I think the first  thing you need to do is talk to the  people who have done that job before so  it sounds like Dave is probably your  best bet and as the owner I know he's  probably running around with a million  thing to do but if he's done that job  that has gone out and he knows how he  wants a certain thing done then he's  probably the best person to sit down and  talk to what might be an easy way to do  it is instead of having him right manual  which can be really time-consuming and a  lot of entrepreneurs don't like to do  that you could do in either audio  recording so you could just ask some  questions do an interview or you  couldn't film him on the job so you can  go out to a job with him and say this is  how we cut the grass or this is how we  lay sod or whatever it is that you want  to lay out your systems and procedures  for and having those videos could be  really instructional for new people who  are coming on board to just watch  exactly how it's been done and not  always then have to use up Dave's time a  quick example my wife runs a salsa  dancing business and she started off  being the only instructor  15 7 days a week and was burning out and  you know she was the only one doing what  she was doing and it was hard for her to  find people to come on and teach under  her and as she expanded her business she  realized you know she can only be one  person for so long to grow I have to go  beyond myself and she created videos  that laid out exactly what she did in  every class so that when she brought on  her new instructors they could watch the  videos and teach it the same way that  she had done and it doesn't mean that  she has to go and teach them every  single time herself so it could be the  same example with Dave where he can go  and greet these videos and you can just  do really rough cuts it doesn't have to  be professionally edited it's only for  internal purposes and maybe it's up to  you to then kind of cut out the best  parts and and see what's really relevant  and it'll save a lot of time compared to  you know Dave having a writer formal  manual the other option would be to go  and try to find people who have done the  job for the company in the past you said  there's been a lot of turnover maybe you  can find somebody who's who's been  really good in the past and had to leave  for you know whatever reason hopefully  they're still on good terms with the  business and approach them to say now  we're trying to create a video series or  training manual to help people who are  coming into the job you know would you  mind sitting down with us for half a day  or day and you know helping us know if  I'm remembering what you did before on  the job so you have your former  employees come and help you out that's  assuming you know they like the business  and they had a good relationship with  you guys and you can still contact them  but you have to start talking to  somebody who's gone through the process  so start with Dave and he's able to do  it that's great and if Dave doesn't know  then start trying to contact your older  employees and you'll see if they'd be  willing to come back and help out for a  day or half a day and the important  lesson kind of going forward is start  documenting everything now right so if  you're in the office think about all the  things that you're doing on a regular  basis and how can that be documented to  somebody can come in later on and do it  really easily so it could be again just  you know paper documents that you're  writing in a word document you send  people I found that that can work to  some degree but it's better if you can  do some kind of videos as well so if  it's office type of work at my business  we use a software called Camtasia which  will record your screen and a lot  talk into a microphone so people can see  exactly what you're doing and can hear  your voice as you explain what you're  doing so it's a lot better training than  just looking over a word document so  hopefully that will help videos are  definitely great where to go and it can  definitely be you know super low budget  doesn't have to be high production  quality if it's only going to be used  for internal purposes for the sales  compensation it really depends on your  business and your margins so if you have  a high margin business you can afford to  give up a greater percentage when I was  in software sales I had my own software  company we hire salespeople to come work  for us and organizations to come you  know help sell the cell the software for  us and we would give up between 25 and  35 percent commission just no top top  line revenue to give them a huge  incentive to want to go and sell to our  you know potential customers now you  probably couldn't afford that because  you have much higher costs associated  with your business and we do in a  software type of company so you have to  find something that makes sense  depending on your margins usually I mean  it's great to try to get a sales person  comes on a commission only on that  scenario then you know you're not  spending anything until you're actually  getting money coming in the door but  it's usually pretty hard to track sales  people to do commission only from the  start there's usually a ramp-up period  it could be you know a couple months if  it's not too difficult a business and it  shouldn't take you know too long for  people to jump into but you want to  support your sales people and you know  you want to try to make that investment  so say it's three months to get ramped  up to start making sales then you know  you have to support them with some kind  of salary or you can see a draw against  commission so that you know their  eventual goal is to be commissioned only  but you got to get them to that point  where they're able to support themselves  that will help you attract a higher  caliber sales person because they show  that you know you're really believing in  them and you're a solid company and  you're going to help support them  through those first couple months where  they're still learning it is a financial  outlay so that's the kind of downside  but you're more likely to get highly you  know talented people instead of the ones  who will come on just for you know  Commission and they'll probably leave  you really quickly when they can  get another job that pays faster so you  don't necessarily just want to go for  those one hundred percent commission  people reckon the start so hopefully  that helps you know I feel too bad for  situation but but hopefully there's some  information here that can help you you  know get that business going again and  you know reignite Dave's passion for the  